WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.218
Command of Evidence: Quantitative

00:00:03.598 --> 00:00:07.531
Accurate AND relevant. Most wrong answers are only one.

00:00:08.731 --> 00:00:09.730
How to spot it

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:11.387
A table or graph

00:00:11.937 --> 00:00:14.726
The question says “uses data… to support”

00:00:15.276 --> 00:00:19.060
It’s testing: connecting data to one specific claim

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:21.055
The method

00:00:21.455 --> 00:00:23.684
Step 1: Read the claim first

00:00:23.964 --> 00:00:25.639
What comparison does it need?

00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:28.568
Step 2: Then read the graphic

00:00:28.848 --> 00:00:30.830
Title, labels, units.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.416
Step 3: Is it accurate?

00:00:33.696 --> 00:00:34.747
Check the numbers.

00:00:35.297 --> 00:00:37.269
Step 4: Is it relevant?

00:00:37.549 --> 00:00:38.905
Does it prove THIS claim?

00:00:39.455 --> 00:00:40.565
In a few words

00:00:40.715 --> 00:00:43.561
Claim first. Accurate. Relevant.

00:00:44.761 --> 00:00:45.739
Data rules

00:00:46.139 --> 00:00:47.507
Accurate and relevant

00:00:47.787 --> 00:00:49.191
The answer must be both.

00:00:49.741 --> 00:00:51.098
Match the comparison

00:00:51.378 --> 00:00:53.221
The right groups AND the right years.

00:00:53.771 --> 00:00:54.789
Check the units

00:00:55.069 --> 00:00:58.885
Percent vs number. Thousands vs millions.

00:00:59.435 --> 00:01:01.026
A change needs two points

00:01:01.306 --> 00:01:02.858
One year can’t show a trend.

00:01:04.058 --> 00:01:05.099
Let’s try one

00:01:05.504 --> 00:01:09.599
A student is researching how city residents get to work.

00:01:09.599 --> 00:01:15.839
She claims that in Portland, the share of commuters who bicycle more than doubled between 2000 and

00:01:15.839 --> 00:01:20.530
2015, while the share who walked remained nearly unchanged.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:26.057
Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the student’s claim?

00:01:26.707 --> 00:01:32.448
The claim has two parts: biking more than doubled, and walking stayed nearly the same.

00:01:32.998 --> 00:01:40.457
The table: biking went from 1.8 to 7.0. Walking went from 5.2 to 5.6.

00:01:41.007 --> 00:01:47.724
A, C, and D are accurate, but each one misses part of the claim. Only B shows both.

00:01:48.924 --> 00:01:49.919
Common traps

00:01:50.319 --> 00:01:51.818
Accurate but irrelevant

00:01:52.098 --> 00:01:54.621
True about the graph. Wrong claim.

00:01:55.171 --> 00:01:56.103
Half the claim

00:01:56.383 --> 00:01:57.802
Shows one trend of two.

00:01:59.002 --> 00:02:01.620
Command of Evidence: Quantitative

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.746
Claim first. Accurate. Relevant.

00:02:05.296 --> 00:02:06.069
Spot it

00:02:06.269 --> 00:02:09.361
Table or graph + “uses data to support”

00:02:09.911 --> 00:02:10.619
Steps

00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:12.619
1. Read the claim first

00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:15.856
2. Read the title, labels, units

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:17.408
3. Accurate?

00:02:17.688 --> 00:02:19.627
4. Relevant to THIS claim?

00:02:20.177 --> 00:02:20.930
Remember

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:22.497
Accurate AND relevant

00:02:22.777 --> 00:02:24.506
Right groups + right years

00:02:24.786 --> 00:02:26.767
Check units (% vs number)

00:02:27.047 --> 00:02:28.638
A change needs two points

00:02:29.188 --> 00:02:29.793
Trap

00:02:29.993 --> 00:02:32.098
True about the graph, wrong claim

00:02:32.848 --> 00:02:36.543
Pause the video now, and copy this page into your notes.
